    "description": "Family life in Iran encompasses living arrangements, relationships across households, everyday cooperation, and the transmission of customs. Census evidence and social research describe changing domestic circumstances, while also showing why household size alone cannot measure family solidarity.",
    "tags": ["Family life", "Households", "Intergenerational relationships", "Everyday culture", "Care", "Lifestyle change"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T09:56:50Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T09:56:50Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Family and Lifestyle in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "An evidence-based introduction to Iranian households, intergenerational support, shared traditions, and the limits of broad claims about family life."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "family-beyond-residence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Family extends beyond the people at home",
        "content": "Understanding Iranian family life requires separating residence from relationship. A household describes a domestic living arrangement; a family network can connect parents, children, siblings, grandparents, and relatives by marriage who live at different addresses. Research on older people in Tehran explicitly includes relatives outside the household in its definition of family. Someone living separately from adult children can therefore remain part of an active network of practical and emotional support. [s1]\n\nThis distinction shapes the questions worth asking. Who shares a home? Who visits? Who helps when someone needs care? Who feels supported? These questions examine different aspects of domestic life. A count of residents cannot, by itself, answer all of them. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "historical-change",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Changing circumstances and older expectations",
        "content": "In a historical study published in 2002, Marie Ladier-Fouladi describes the patriarchal extended family as an influential earlier model: related married couples and their children belonged to a larger unit under male authority. The family also organized welfare, work connections, marriage, and assistance across the life course. This is a historical analytical model, not a description to apply automatically to contemporary Iranian homes. [s2]\n\nLadier-Fouladi connects changes in family organization with wage employment, urbanization, education, and the development of public services. Her account provides a way to think about lifestyle change through institutions and material circumstances. It also highlights evidence problems: earlier census tabulations did not always make it possible to distinguish the family units within a household. A simple story of extended families being replaced by independent individuals would overlook both measurement limits and relationships maintained across residences. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "census-context",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What the census can establish",
        "content": "The Statistical Center of Iran's selected census results provide separate information about population, marital status, and household size. These categories should be read independently. Marital-status tables describe people in a specified age group; household averages describe domestic units. Neither directly measures affection, decision-making, or the distribution of unpaid work. [s3]\n\nThe booklet's household-size table indicates smaller average households between its two census observations, but its heading includes an apparent percentage-unit error. Because the unit requires confirmation against the underlying census documentation, the values are not republished here as a dataset. The broader editorial point remains essential: precise definitions matter before a number becomes an explanation of social change. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "care-and-daily-life",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Cooperation is made of ordinary tasks",
        "content": "Interview research on older parents receiving help at home makes family cooperation tangible. Participants described assistance with cleaning, shopping, repairs, expenses, healthcare, and using technology, alongside companionship and participation in social occasions. These accounts show that support involves maintaining everyday independence as well as responding to illness. [s4]\n\nThe research also cautions against an idealized picture of effortless family care. Its discussion identifies pressures on caregivers and the importance of supporting them. As a qualitative study, it explains experiences and forms of assistance; it cannot establish how common each arrangement is across Iran. Family obligation, available time, and practical capacity should therefore be examined separately. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "transmitting-customs",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Traditions are learned through participation",
        "content": "Celebrations offer another view of family relationships. UNESCO describes Yalda, the gathering on the final night of autumn, as an occasion when families meet at elders' homes, share foods, tell stories, recite poetry, and play games or music. Its account identifies learning within families alongside transmission through publications, broadcasting, educational activities, and social media. Domestic tradition can consequently involve both face-to-face participation and wider cultural channels. [s5]\n\nEveryday food preparation also links family with community. UNESCO's account of shared flatbread traditions describes relatives dividing preparation tasks, neighbors cooperating in rural settings, and traditional bakeries participating in production. The account spans several countries, including Iran, and identifies both shared practices and local differences. It illustrates why household life should be considered alongside neighborhood cooperation and skilled work. [s6]",
        "source_ids": ["s5", "s6"]
      },
      {
        "id": "exploring-the-branches",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Different questions need different evidence",
        "content": "The pages in this branch separate family structures, intergenerational relationships, changing lifestyles, youth culture, single and family life, apartment living, and rural lifestyles. Read together, they should connect demographic patterns with lived experience while preserving differences between populations and places. The available evidence here supports an introduction to households, care, and cultural transmission; it does not yet support a comprehensive account of contemporary youth, housing routines, or rural domestic life.",
        "source_ids": []
      }
    ],
