    "description": "Iranian family structures can be examined through kinship, shared residence, authority, and the exchange of support. Historical extended households and contemporary relationships across separate homes illustrate why no single domestic arrangement can stand for every Iranian family.",
    "tags": ["Family structures", "Kinship", "Households", "Extended families", "Social support", "Domestic history"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T09:56:50Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T09:56:50Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Iranian Family Structures: Households and Kinship | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand Iranian family structures through historical households, kinship beyond shared residence, and research on family support."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "dimensions-of-structure",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Residence, kinship, and support",
        "content": "Family structure becomes clearer when several questions are kept distinct. Kinship concerns relationships between people; household composition concerns who lives together; authority concerns who makes decisions; support concerns what relatives do for one another. Research in Tehran includes parents, children, spouses, siblings, grandparents, grandchildren, and relatives by marriage within a family network, including people living elsewhere. A household roster captures only part of that network. [s1]\n\nThese distinctions prevent misleading comparisons. An extended family network need not occupy one building. Conversely, the presence of several generations at one address does not establish how decisions, resources, or care are distributed. The Tehran research examines living arrangements, contact, relationship quality, and support as separate variables rather than treating residence as a complete description of family life. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "historical-households",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The historical extended household",
        "content": "Encyclopaedia Iranica's account of Qajar-period households describes a compound that could contain a couple, married sons and their families, and unmarried daughters. Wealthy establishments might also include servants, retainers, visiting relatives, and other dependents. Such a household was therefore more complex than a family tree: some residents were connected through employment and patronage. [s2]\n\nThe account describes authority concentrated in the oldest male and an influential domestic position for the senior woman. It also emphasizes fluid membership, as servants and visitors moved between residences. These details help explain the historical relationship between architecture, hierarchy, and domestic organization. They should not be transferred wholesale to present-day families or treated as evidence that every household in the Qajar period had the same resources and composition. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "networks-across-addresses",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A separate home can remain connected",
        "content": "A Tehran survey conducted in 2015 and published in 2023 found associations between family characteristics and older people's reported support. Its discussion distinguishes physical proximity from actual contact: nearby children did not necessarily visit more, and contact mattered for assistance. The finding supports a careful interpretation of separate residence rather than an assumption that it means family withdrawal. The study was cross-sectional and limited to community-resident older people in Tehran, so it cannot establish nationwide patterns or prove causation. [s1]\n\nQualitative research adds detail about what these relationships can involve. Older parents described children helping with domestic tasks, repairs, finances, and technology. Some accounts linked assistance to maintaining a safe, functioning home. These examples show how relatives can contribute to an older person's household without all becoming members of it. The interviews explain forms of cooperation rather than their national prevalence. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "transmission-and-belonging",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Family relationships also carry knowledge",
        "content": "Family structure is not only a question of residence or care. UNESCO's description of Yalda records gatherings at elders' homes where storytelling, poetry, food, games, and gifts connect participants. It identifies informal learning within families as a means of transmitting the tradition. The gathering makes relationships visible through participation, but it does not reveal whether those participants share a permanent home. [s4]\n\nThe most useful account of an Iranian family therefore states its setting and the relationship being examined. A historical compound, a contemporary home, a wider kin network, and a festive gathering describe different social arrangements. Keeping those categories explicit allows continuity and change to be discussed without making one household model a universal national portrait. [s1][s2][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s4"]
      }
    ],
